.Dd September 25, 2026
.Dt MOUNT_MINIXFS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mount_minixfs
.Nd mount a MINIX file system image with FUSE
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Oo
.Fl w
.Op Fl u Cm always | sync | Ar seconds
.Oc
.Op Fl M Ar size : Ns Ar heads : Ns Ar side
.Op Ar FUSE options
.Ar image
.Ar mountpoint
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
mounts the MINIX file system in
.Ar image
on
.Ar mountpoint
through the high-level FUSE interface, which libfuse on Linux and
.Fx
and librefuse on
.Nx
and MINIX 3 provide.
Every version, V1, V2 and V3, in either byte order, and the file systems
of Minix-vmd are read; see
.Xr minixfs 5 .
.Pp
The mount is read-only unless
.Fl w
is given, and single-threaded: the options
.Fl s
and
.Fl o Ar ro ,
or
.Fl o Ar rw,default_permissions
with
.Fl w ,
are always given to FUSE.
Inode numbers, modes, link counts, owners, times and device numbers are
those of the image.
The image is opened before FUSE takes over, so a relative path works.
Other options, such as
.Fl f
to stay in the foreground or
.Fl o Ar allow_other ,
are passed on to FUSE.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl w
Mount the file system for writing.
Files, directories, symbolic links, hard links, pipes and devices can be
made, removed, renamed and changed.
The kernel checks permissions against the modes and owners in the image.
A new file belongs to the caller, but takes the group of its directory
where that is set-group-ID, as a new directory takes the bit too, or
where the inode cannot hold the group of the caller, as in V1, whose
inodes keep the group in a byte.
The mtime and ctime of what changes are set; atimes are not.
A file removed while it is open keeps its inode and its data, with no
name, until it is closed, and then goes.
Meanwhile libfuse, of Linux and
.Fx ,
hides it under a name of its own,
.Ql .fuse_hidden
and 16 hexadecimal digits: where names are of 14 characters, which cannot
hold that, the name is kept in memory and not in the directory; where
they are longer, it is in the directory until the file is closed, unless
.Cm hide=memory
is given.
.Pp
While the file system is mounted,
.Ar image
is locked against other programs that write it, such as
.Nm fsck_minixfs Fl y ,
and its clean mark is away, as Linux and MINIX 3 take it away; unmounting
puts it back.
A file system that is not marked clean, or that has the flex directories
of Minix-vmd, which this cannot write, is mounted read-only with a
warning.
.It Fl u Cm always | sync | Ar seconds
When the bit maps, which say which inodes and zones are in use, go to
the image: at
.Xr fsync 2
and unmount, as fuse2fs does
.Pq Cm sync , No the default ;
as they change, as ntfs-3g does
.Pq Cm always ;
or, besides, with the first change after
.Ar seconds
have gone since they last did, as
.Xr update 8
of MINIX writes everything every 30 seconds.
Everything else goes to the image as it changes.
If
.Nm
dies, the image is left not marked clean, and with
.Cm sync
the maps may be behind the inodes;
.Nm fsck_minixfs Fl y
puts them right.
.It Fl M Ar size : Ns Ar heads : Ns Ar side
Take an image that holds the file system in the tracks of one side of a
disk only, as
.Xr minixfs 1
does.
.It Fl o Ar options
Besides the options of FUSE, the comma-separated list may hold those of
.Nm ,
as
.Xr mount 8
gives them:
.Cm rw
for
.Fl w ,
.Cm ro
to take that back,
.Cm update= Ns Ar X
for
.Fl u Ar X ,
.Cm tracks= Ns Ar X
for
.Fl M Ar X ,
and
.Cm hide=memory
to keep the names that libfuse hides files removed while open under in
memory also where names are longer than 14 characters, or
.Cm hide=disk ,
the default, to leave them in the directory there.
.El
.Pp
The file system is unmounted with
.Ql fusermount3 -u Ar mountpoint
on Linux, or with
.Xr umount 8
on the BSDs.
.Ss MINIX 3
On MINIX 3,
.Nm
is a service that
.Xr mount 8
starts, not a command.
It is built with
.Ql make fuse-minix
and installed as
.Pa /usr/pkg/service/minixfs ,
and
.Pa /etc/system.conf
is given an entry
.Ql service minixfs ,
a copy of that of ntfs-3g.
The image is mounted from a vnd device, with the options in
.Fl o :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# vndconfig vnd0 usr.img
# mount -t minixfs -o rw /dev/vnd0 /mnt
# umount /mnt
# vndconfig -u vnd0
.Ed
.Pp
Run by hand,
.Nm
shows how to mount and exits with 2.
A service has no standard error, so its warnings are not seen, and the
lock is on the vnd device, not on
.Ar image .
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits with 0 when the file system has been unmounted, 1 if the image
cannot be opened, FUSE fails or the image cannot be written back, and 2
for a usage error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Look at an image:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ mount_minixfs usr.img /mnt
$ ls -l /mnt
$ fusermount3 -u /mnt
.Ed
.Pp
Copy a tree into an image, with the maps written as they change:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ mount_minixfs -w -u always usr.img /mnt
$ cp -R src /mnt
$ fusermount3 -u /mnt
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr minixfs 1 ,
.Xr minixfs 5 ,
.Xr fsck_minixfs 8 ,
.Xr newfs_minixfs 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Shoichi Fukusaka
.Sh BUGS
On MINIX 3, writing is not reliable, as libpuffs gets the new nodes
wrong: a symbolic link made through the mount loses its target, a pipe
is made with no valid type, and a character device is made a block
device.
.Pp
On
.Nx ,
a directory made in a set-group-ID directory takes its group but not
the set-group-ID bit, as librefuse sets the mode that the kernel gave
once the directory is made.
.Pp
On
.Nx
10.0 and later, looking up
.Ql \&..
in a directory right below the root fails, and the mount misbehaves
from then on, as it does for every file system served through
librefuse; see
.Pa docs/netbsd-refuse-dotdot.md
in the source.
.Pp
On
.Nx
and MINIX 3, librefuse passes on only the first of the opens of a file
that overlap, and says a file is closed at the first close.
A file removed while open is then kept until the unmount, not until it
is closed; and a file opened twice, closed once and then removed goes at
once, so that writing through the other descriptor changes a free inode,
or a file that took it.
.Pp
On
.Fx ,
.Xr ln 1
through the mount fails with EIO, although the link is made: libfuse
answers a hard link with a node number of its own for the new name, and
the fusefs of
.Fx
takes an answer with another node number than that of the file for a
failure.
.Pp
Where the names libfuse hides files under are kept in memory, a file
that is open and that the user renames to a name of that form is taken
for one that libfuse hides: the name goes at the unmount, and the file
with it unless it has another.
A file that is not open is renamed as to any other name.
.Pp
libfuse gives the link count of a file removed while open one too high
for a while after its mode, owner or times are changed through it: it
takes the name it hides the file under off the count it gives for
.Xr fstat 2 ,
but not off the one it gives back for the change, which the kernel keeps.
